Timing-dependent flake in Node. Save /workspace/queue.mjs:

export class JobQueue {
  constructor() { this.jobs = []; this.done = []; this.running = false; }
  push(job) { this.jobs.push(job); this.#kick(); }
  #kick() {
    if (this.running) return;
    this.running = true;
    setTimeout(() => this.#drain(), 0);
  }
  async #drain() {
    while (this.jobs.length > 0) {
      const job = this.jobs.shift();
      const result = await job();
      this.done.push(result);
    }
    this.running = false;
  }
}

and /workspace/queue.test.mjs:

import test from "node:test";
import assert from "node:assert";
import { JobQueue } from "./queue.mjs";

test("processes both jobs", async () => {
  const q = new JobQueue();
  q.push(async () => { await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, Math.random() * 20)); return "a"; });
  q.push(async () => "b");
  await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 25));
  assert.deepEqual(q.done, ["a", "b"]);
});

The 25ms sleep sometimes loses the race against the random 0-20ms job plus scheduling jitter. Reproduce: run node --test 20 times in a shell loop and count failures (crank Math.random() * 20 up to * 30 temporarily if your machine is too fast to flake, then put it back). The fix is structural, not a bigger sleep: give JobQueue a completion signal, e.g. an idle() method returning a promise that resolves when the queue empties (resolve pending waiters at the end of #drain), then rewrite the test to await q.idle() with NO arbitrary timeouts and no Math.random (make the first job deterministic 10ms). Add tests: jobs pushed while draining still run before idle resolves; a rejecting job does not wedge the queue (decide semantics: record the error in q.done as {error} and continue; test it). 20/20 green in the loop afterward.

